        "Audit1897 intentionally removes219 authored definitions/322 static rows across48 selected sources, including23 whole suites. The profile-state selector loses fixture/canonical equality and soak inventories, historical compatibility-acceptance metadata matrices, setup/default/bootstrap/migration inventories, backup-name/rotation branch inventories, raw-SQL child crash scripts that reimplement writes, private domain readers/equality/serialization, fragment-forgery matrices and parsed-snapshot plumbing. Removed command/error-message and injected access inventories are not replacement-validated. Retained independent SQL fencing, trigger rollback, production crash export/recovery and durable byte outcomes do not establish the lost alarms. Static totals use the macOS copy-boundary superset; no new native, WSL/SSH/mobile, rendered UI or cross-platform run is supplied. Exact accepted per-definition losses are recorded in sealed1897 reviews; all current selectors/assertionRefs and the unchanged2026-10-05 macOS E2E raw receipt remain. No equivalent replacement or fresh unit gate evidence is claimed.",
        "Audit1904 intentionally loses these direct profile-state source alarms: 'refuses rather than replaces something else at the backup path'; 'does nothing for an empty batch'; 'shares one process with absolute arguments and the per-file timeout budget'; 'bounds a shared process when many large artifacts are retained'; 'isolates %s/accepted=%s through corruption and WAL reopen'; 'bundles one process-host source graph into the isolated recovery fixture'; 'refuses readiness when a required worker artifact is absent'; 'refuses unknown and escaping IDs without falling back to the active profile'; 'rejects reused input before Store context installation (secret=%s)'; 'reads established storage once with retained JSON=%s'; 'consumes a parsed startup snapshot once, including an empty revision'; 'rejects initial state without its authority or alongside migration input'; 'keeps any other failed commit indeterminate even after SQLite rolled back'; 'joins an aborted writer without reporting a close request that was never sent'; 'keeps the SQLite failure behind a database open error across the writer boundary'; 'carries a real SQLite error code from a failed open'; 'keeps a cause-less open error cause-less'; 'refuses a versioned export with revision %s for an admitted revision of one'; 'refuses a revision jump at an unchanged-state fence'; 'refuses an initialization acknowledgement for a different revision'; 'leaves startup failure reporting to the startup caller'; 'does not report saving stopped after a recoverable request failure or clean close'; 'retries the first failure at once, then backs off up to a minute'; 'forgets the backoff once a reopen succeeds'; 'lets one attempt through a backoff for a final flush'; 'drops a pending bypass once a reopen succeeds'; 'permanently retires bootstrap authority and refuses subsequent calls'; 'aborts an active request without treating the pending write as rolled back'; 'resolves flat and shared-chunk entry layouts'. Retained production SQLite recovery, durable bytes, fencing and worker callbacks do not establish those exact lost alarms. Sources and accepted reasons are in sealed1904 reviews; no equivalent replacement or fresh integrated unit gate run is claimed.",
