      "coverageNotes": "Audit3359 keeps two restored-editor-owner-reparent cases covering coupled dirty-editor/draft/preview/main-group migration and hot-exit serialization/hydration. The subscribed activation, collision refusal and mounted watch replacement/destination-event correlation alarms are intentionally lost without equivalent replacement. The complete compound dated receipt is retired verbatim. With no active receipt, protection and covered scope are cleared and prospective command/test-file/assertion selectors are retired; their complete originals and prior red/green/flake accounts remain historical in knownGaps. The retained reparent cases and unchanged save-lifecycle source remain reachable through broad unit execution, not active evidence for this gate. No fresh provider, filesystem restart, historical-import, mobile, mixed-version or cross-platform validation is claimed. Deterministic store/controller tests cover local, direct-SSH, paired-runtime identity, folder workspaces, queue quiescence, stale route and connection-generation rejection, authoritative active-workspace projection, owner-derived IDs, drafts and editor maps, tab/group placement, exact watch replacement, change/delete/rename correlation, explicit save, autosave, collision refusal without activation reconciliation, provenance, and restart hot-exit persistence. Live headed/headless paired-runtime and post-establish Electron IPC remain uncollected. The test-value audit removed the standalone runtime-workspace file-route suite. Its direct same-host sibling, folder-root, exact-SSH, outside-path and directory-routing alarms are lost. Retained editor lifecycle contracts do not establish equivalent selector coverage; historical run commands and results remain historical. The test-value audit narrows restored-editor-owner-reparent.test.ts by removing standalone unrelated-explorer-reveal, duplicate-tab repair/refusal, clean/dirty collision-table, watcher-target projection, folder-workspace context and direct-SSH provenance examples. Their exact scalar/ID/reference, selector and route/generation-field alarms are intentionally lost. The dirty collision variant did not assert preservation of its destination draft, and the main-owned duplicate-tab branch checked a synthetic missing-group refusal rather than duplicate repair. Remaining authored cases still check coupled dirty-state migration, subscribed activation, hot-exit roundtrip, rejection without destination reconciliation, mounted watch replacement and destination event correlation. They are not equivalent replacements for the deleted alarms. Historical executed commands, counts and results remain unchanged; no new live host, process restart or platform evidence is claimed.",
