      "coveredProviders": ["remote-runtime"],
      "coverageNotes": "The retained fake-clock scheduler cases check bounded exhaustion, rejected attempts continuing to exhaustion, in-flight cancellation, and one queued replacement chain. Direct transient-success counted-delay, healthy-budget reset, and pending-cancellation cases were removed. Existing lifecycle and paired-runtime coverage remain separate and were not re-reviewed as equivalent replacements in this audit. The historical headless paired journey disconnects the real runtime, shows Reconnect, reconnects the same host, and requires a new frame. Headed paired-server, Windows, sleep/wake, and real network-partition evidence remain uncollected.",
      "motivatingLinks": [
        "https://github.com/stablyai/orca/pull/12787",
        "https://linear.app/stably/issue/STA-3483"
      ],
      "invariant": "A current remote browser stream that closes must retry transient failures on one counted, capped backoff chain. Cancellation, pane or runtime supersession, missing pages, and permanent failures stop that chain without stale state mutation. A stream that stays healthy refills the budget; a silent or flapping stream cannot retry forever. Exhaustion and every non-retrying failure leave an actionable Reconnect control, and manual reconnect creates a fresh stream without resurrecting stale subscriptions.",
      "oracle": "The retained direct scheduler oracles require finite exhaustion, continuation after rejected attempts, cancellation of in-flight ownership without re-arming, and one queued replacement chain. Existing lifecycle assertions separately describe stale pane/operation/stream/page/runtime refusal, liveness, and manual recovery; they are not claimed equivalent to removed direct scheduler cases. The paired-runtime oracle paints a remote frame, disconnects its owning runtime, observes the bounded-retry notice and Reconnect control, reconnects, and requires a different frame object URL.",
